{
  "id": "xobb67z",
  "title": "Chapter 7: Electricity and Magnetism",
  "order": 7,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "35hi4da",
      "content": "Electricity and magnetism are closely related branches of physics. Electricity deals with the flow of electric charges, while magnetism deals with the magnetic properties of materials and magnetic fields produced by electric currents."
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "SI Unit of Current",
          "value": "Ampere (A)",
          "icon": "check"
        },
        {
          "label": "SI Unit of Voltage",
          "value": "Volt (V)",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "SI Unit of Resistance",
          "value": "Ohm (Ω)",
          "icon": "award"
        },
        {
          "label": "Core Equation",
          "value": "V = I × R",
          "icon": "book"
        }
      ],
      "id": "9yy6n1e"
    },
    {
      "id": "vrxijaf",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Electricity"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Electric current is defined as the rate of flow of electric charges (electrons) through a conductor.",
        "Voltage or potential difference drives electric current and is measured in Volts.",
        "Ohm’s Law dictates that current flowing through a conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference across its ends.",
        "Electric power is the rate at which electrical energy is transferred in an electric circuit (P = V × I).",
        "Magnets always exist as dipoles (North and South poles); isolated magnetic monopoles do not exist.",
        "An electromagnet's strength can be controlled by altering the current or increasing the number of coil turns."
      ],
      "id": "ijy9gjm"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "50ujpth",
      "content": "Electricity is the flow of electric charge through a conductor. Electric current is the movement of electrons through a conductor such as copper wire."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "aze59lp",
      "content": "• Electric charge is a fundamental property of matter."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "u5v2zfg",
      "content": "• Electric current is the flow of electric charge."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "isqr1d2",
      "content": "• The SI unit of electric current is Ampere (A)."
    },
    {
      "id": "q18kkk4",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. Electric Current"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "29solr3",
      "content": "Electric current is defined as the rate of flow of electric charge."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "spgl7wi",
      "content": "Formula: I = Q / t"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "70rqgty",
      "content": "Where:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "wqftkp2",
      "content": "I = Electric current"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "pg2y5cs",
      "content": "Q = Electric charge"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "d5597mn",
      "content": "t = Time"
    },
    {
      "id": "mj3anig",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "3. Voltage"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "uztw2eh",
      "content": "Voltage is the potential difference between two points in an electric circuit."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gylfbt3",
      "content": "SI Unit of Voltage = Volt (V)"
    },
    {
      "id": "d8px8bq",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "4. Resistance"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Ohm’s Law states that electric current is directly proportional to voltage and inversely proportional to resistance.",
      "id": "aw8d8s2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cfba5e7",
      "content": "Resistance is the opposition offered by a conductor to the flow of electric current."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "6qv5u3g",
      "content": "SI Unit of Resistance = Ohm (Ω)"
    },
    {
      "id": "3lohfyg",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "5. Ohm’s Law"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "06egeex",
      "content": "Ohm’s Law states that the current flowing through a conductor is directly proportional to the voltage across it, provided temperature remains constant."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "ijm7715",
      "content": "Formula: V = IR"
    },
    {
      "id": "rbdzp44",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "6. Electric Power"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ryryc7x",
      "content": "Electric power is the rate at which electrical energy is consumed."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "g5tgcoo",
      "content": "Formula: P = VI"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "5ggihfe",
      "content": "SI Unit = Watt (W)"
    },
    {
      "id": "f3w82r0",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "7. Types of Electric Circuits"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Parameter",
        "Series Circuit",
        "Parallel Circuit"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Current Flow",
          "Same current through all components",
          "Current divides among parallel paths"
        ],
        [
          "Voltage Distribution",
          "Voltage drops across each resistor",
          "Same voltage across all components"
        ],
        [
          "Total Resistance",
          "Req = R1 + R2 + ... (Increases overall resistance)",
          "1/Req = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + ... (Decreases overall resistance)"
        ],
        [
          "Fault Impact",
          "If one component fails, the entire circuit stops working",
          "If one branch fails, other branches continue operating"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "5vnxrzg"
    },
    {
      "id": "77r1s9i",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Circuit Type</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Description</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Series Circuit</p>",
            "<p>Components connected in a single path</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
